---
const portraitSections = [
  'identity',
  'communication',
  'trust and autonomy',
  'active context',
  'lessons',
  'edges',
  'episodes',
  'voice',
  'decisions',
  'context history',
  'landscapes',
];

const strokeParts = ['when', 'shortcut', 'steps', 'undo', 'traps'];

const brushParts = [
  'what it is',
  'can reach',
  'cannot',
  'pigment and cabinet',
  'granted by and when',
  'how to check it',
  'how to take it away',
  'used by',
];

const landscapeParts = [
  'what it is and who cares',
  'how it works',
  'traps',
  'decisions',
  'open threads',
  'where things live',
];
---

<section id="how" class="border-b border-border/40 bg-panel/30">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 py-24">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-muted mb-6">
      02 · The shape
    </p>
    <h2 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-ink tracking-tight mb-8">
      Four kinds of files.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-ink-dim leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mb-12">
      A portrait is a picture of a being. A landscape is a picture of the
      ground it stands on. A stroke is how it does one thing there. A
      brush is what lets it make the mark. A pigment loads the brush, and
      a cabinet keeps the pigment locked.
    </p>

    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 text-ink-dim">
      <div class="rounded-md border border-border/60 bg-bg/40 p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent mb-2">The portrait</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ember mb-4">~/.claude/essence/portrait.md</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">
          One markdown file. It tells each new session who it is and how to
          work with its person. It has eleven sections.
        </p>
        <ol class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4">
          {
            portraitSections.map((s, i) => (
              <li class="font-mono text-[11px] rounded border border-border/70 px-2 py-1 text-ink-dim">
                <span class="text-muted">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span> {s}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          The identity section is written in the first person. The person
          and the agent write it together.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-md border border-border/60 bg-bg/40 p-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent mb-2">The landscapes</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ember mb-4">~/.claude/essence/landscapes/</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">
          One markdown file per area of work. The template suggests six
          parts, plus short lists of its strokes and brushes. Use what fits.
        </p>
        <ol class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4">
          {
            landscapeParts.map((s, i) => (
              <li class="font-mono text-[11px] rounded border border-border/70 px-2 py-1 text-ink-dim">
                <span class="text-muted">{i + 1}</span> {s}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          A landscape says what is true now, in under 30 KB. It holds no
          history: a separate running diary, the handoff, keeps that.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-md border border-border/60 bg-bg/40 p-6 md:col-span-2">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent mb-2">The strokes</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ember mb-4">~/.claude/essence/strokes/</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">
          One markdown file per procedure, in five parts. Its shortcut is a
          tested script that does the steps. Until that script exists, the
          stroke says "Shortcut: none yet".
        </p>
        <ol class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4">
          {
            strokeParts.map((s, i) => (
              <li class="font-mono text-[11px] rounded border border-border/70 px-2 py-1 text-ink-dim">
                <span class="text-muted">{i + 1}</span> {s}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          The script does the steps the same way every time and refuses to
          guess. A shortcut and a hook are the same kind of thing: a script.
          A hook is a script the harness starts by itself at a set moment. A
          shortcut is a script the agent starts when it follows a stroke.
          The stroke holds what a script cannot: when to use it, where
          to stop and ask, what went wrong before. Each stroke belongs to a
          landscape, and the agent reaches it through that landscape. The
          author named them in November 2026, when the first one was
          written.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-md border border-border/60 bg-bg/40 p-6 md:col-span-2">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent mb-2">The brushes</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ember mb-4">~/.claude/essence/brushes/</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">
          One markdown file per ability: a login, a role, a token or a tool.
          Each has eight parts.
        </p>
        <ol class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4">
          {
            brushParts.map((s, i) => (
              <li class="font-mono text-[11px] rounded border border-border/70 px-2 py-1 text-ink-dim">
                <span class="text-muted">{i + 1}</span> {s}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A stroke is a set of instructions, not a capability. You can know a
          stroke perfectly, and without the brush nothing happens. So the
          permissions live on the brush, the only part that touches the world.
          A stroke names the brush it needs. Each landscape lists the brushes
          its work uses, and the agent checks a brush before it promises
          anything that depends on it.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Without them, an agent guesses what it can do. It says it cannot
          change a setting its login can change, and it promises a change no
          credential it holds can make. Each brush gives one read-only command
          that proves it works.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed mt-4">
          Some rules ride on a brush, such as "read this platform's current
          docs first, they change fast". The rule sits at the top of the
          brush, not in every landscape. A check makes sure each landscape
          that involves the platform lists its brush, and that the brush
          still carries the rule.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-md border border-border/60 bg-bg/40 p-6 md:col-span-2">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent mb-2">Pigments and cabinets</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ember mb-4">~/.claude/essence/cabinets.md</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The security part. A brush makes no mark until it is loaded. The
          pigment is the secret that loads it: a token, a password, a key.
          The cabinet is the locked place pigments are kept: a
          password-manager vault. Painters kept costly pigments, such as
          ultramarine ground from lapis, locked away and measured out for one
          job at a time.
        </p>
        <ol class="text-sm leading-relaxed list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1 mb-4">
          <li>A pigment goes into a cabinet before it goes anywhere else.</li>
          <li>A brush is loaded only for the job at hand, at run time. The pigment is never written to disk beside the work.</li>
          <li>No Essence file ever holds a pigment. It names only the cabinet and the drawer: the item's name.</li>
          <li>A pigment never passes through the conversation.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Each brush carries two lines, Pigment and Cabinet. One file lists
          every cabinet the agent can open, what it may never hold, and how
          access is taken away.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
